- **Capital Project Stakeholders:** Mining/project CEOs evaluating processing facilities, investors (PE, VC, family offices, sovereign wealth) funding industrial infrastructure, government program managers (DOE, DOD, EXIM, SBA) allocating grants or loans for domestic production capacity. These people don't use the simulation tool directly but they ARE the audience for the TEA output — they need feasibility answers to commit capital. Events dominated by this persona type should score as ICP-rich, not ICP-poor.
- **Deep Tech Founders:** Founders scaling physical/biological processes in Roebling's verticals (bio, food/ag, chemicals, minerals, energy, decarb, materials). They need process simulation and TEA to raise capital, plan facilities, and make scale-up decisions. They are direct users/buyers. Investors at the same events often serve as channel partners by introducing Roebling to their portfolio companies.

### What Roebling Is
AI-enabled process simulation and techno-economic modeling platform for industrial infrastructure projects. Replaces fragmented feasibility workflows (Excel, Aspen, SuperPro, consultants). Serves FEL-0 through FEL-2. NOT detailed design. NOT a calculator. A decision tool. Roebling's output is what capital project stakeholders — investors, CEOs, boards, government funders — use to decide whether to commit tens or hundreds of millions of dollars to a facility. Events where those capital decisions are being discussed are ICP-rich even if no one in the room has ever opened a process simulator.

### Competitors at Events
If Aspen, SuperPro, SysCAD, or other competitors sponsor an event, note it. It means ICP is present but also means head-to-head visibility. At Roebling's current stage, avoid expensive head-to-head booth competition. Speaking slots are fine.

### Team Constraints
~20 people. No dedicated event staff. Every event pulls engineers or leadership away from product work. Opportunity cost is real. No more than 2-3 events per month without burning people out.

### Budget Philosophy
